## What happened

In **.NET 11**, Apple's DSA support was removed from the macOS cryptography layer. Calling `DSA.Create()` (and any DSA signing/verification) now **throws at runtime on macOS**. Windows and Linux are unaffected — DSA still works there.

## How to fix it

Since you need to **verify legacy signatures**, you have two options:

### Option 1 — Replace DSA with a modern algorithm (recommended)

If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** or **RSA**:

```csharp
// Before (breaks on macOS in .NET 11)
using var dsa = DSA.Create();
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);

// After
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create();
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

This requires re-signing existing data with the new algorithm.

### Option 2 — Platform-gate DSA for legacy verification

If you **must** verify existing DSA signatures and can't re-sign, restrict DSA to platforms that still support it:

```csharp
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
    throw new PlatformNotSupportedException(
        "DSA signature verification is not supported on macOS with .NET 11+.");

using var dsa = DSA.Create();
bool valid = dsa.VerifyData(data, signature, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

Or run verification only on your Linux/Windows nodes.

### Bottom line

DSA is a legacy algorithm. The long-term fix is migrating to **ECDSA** (or RSA). If legacy signatures must be verified on macOS, you'll need to do it on a different platform or use a third-party crypto library that bundles its own DSA implementation.
